{:edb/db-before-t 1440 :edb/db-after-t 1441 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534753 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:53:19.296Z" 13194139534753 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307721 13194139534753 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307773 13194139534753 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307780 13194139534753 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307728 13194139534753 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307735 13194139534753 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307742 13194139534753 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307759 13194139534753 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307749 13194139534753 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307756 13194139534753 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307766 13194139534753 true] [17592186307721 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307722 13194139534753 true] [17592186307721 1089 "q-113756" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307721 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534753 true] [17592186307721 1100 "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307721 1113 #uuid "dfddadf1-89e7-55ef-9cec-6665abaa08ef" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307721 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-2x \\\\  & =2x(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x-1)\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,\\frac{1}{4},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)-1)=8>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =2(\\frac{1}{4})[3(\\frac{1}{4})-1]=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)-1)=4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307723 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307724 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307725 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307726 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307727 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1041 "selection" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307723 13194139534753 true] [17592186307722 1110 #uuid "e1c80fcd-2da6-5e86-a5fc-3f639fbba1a9" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307723 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307723 1111 #uuid "c7ea4f71-8380-5b9c-a8d2-9ec15242eca3" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307724 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307724 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307724 1111 #uuid "8ef8bdae-4107-518d-bd08-5f79ecff4ee1" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307725 1034 "I only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307725 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307725 1111 #uuid "53b286b2-66ec-53b1-ac05-83e6a59eda28" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307726 1034 "III only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307726 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307726 1111 #uuid "873be0bc-2e26-5781-8a45-35c040b74fb0" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307727 1034 "II only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307727 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307727 1111 #uuid "a79408c7-418d-5ad5-a434-cc69fc488420" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307728 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307729 13194139534753 true] [17592186307728 1089 "q-113764" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307728 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534753 true] [17592186307728 1100 "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{9}-9,$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307728 1113 #uuid "78ca0287-8329-5ae9-a1b1-58cbe5481203" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307728 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=9{x}^{8}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9{x}^{8}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =9(-1)^{8}=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =9(1)^{8}=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307730 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307731 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307732 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307733 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307734 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1041 "selection" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307732 13194139534753 true] [17592186307729 1110 #uuid "9ff2fa8f-02a0-520c-8597-a4bc14f6368e" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307730 1034 "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307730 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307730 1111 #uuid "59fd53b8-bf05-559f-b595-63f13fdd2124" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307731 1034 "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307731 1111 #uuid "edce3891-628b-547a-bf0a-d2fb5e5d6d50" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307732 1034 "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307732 1111 #uuid "ff9bd6f7-7638-5b29-9130-d85ab9c7ec72" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307733 1034 "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307733 1111 #uuid "5925efbf-133a-55d9-9f09-52af7b3ca29d" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307734 1034 "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307734 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307734 1111 #uuid "11d8c5ac-17e2-590e-ac86-4d434e7e8e23" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307735 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307736 13194139534753 true] [17592186307735 1089 "q-113767" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307735 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534753 true] [17592186307735 1100 "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307735 1113 #uuid "5d605cec-3072-5304-adcf-70f0f4b40d1d" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307735 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x{e}^{x}-({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{{e}^{0}}=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{(2-1)^{2}}{{e}^{2}}=-{e}^{-2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307737 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307738 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307739 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307740 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307741 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1041 "selection" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307739 13194139534753 true] [17592186307736 1110 #uuid "ec4dd24a-ba72-5563-bdc6-9ebd4dbae0dc" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307737 1034 "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307737 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307737 1111 #uuid "6771e68b-675d-55b3-b173-af4f1bdbdbe0" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307738 1034 "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307738 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307738 1111 #uuid "5d480f4e-48c8-5c71-9e0b-81f67a7f49d1" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307739 1034 "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307739 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307739 1111 #uuid "e9f3acf7-00e8-5f4b-8f46-c1c65af2fcc3" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307740 1034 "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307740 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307740 1111 #uuid "9020d897-268f-501e-9b89-08b73fc5e2db" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307741 1034 "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307741 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307741 1111 #uuid "e704ba8e-dcd5-5574-a2d8-36d17ebaec0e" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307742 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307743 13194139534753 true] [17592186307742 1089 "q-21468" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307742 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534753 true] [17592186307742 1100 "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307742 1113 #uuid "2f415a64-6204-50b1-8aee-a5b869400010" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307742 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307744 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307745 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307746 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307747 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307748 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1041 "selection" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307747 13194139534753 true] [17592186307743 1110 #uuid "e4e0a153-f6a8-5035-b9a0-6164963a381e" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307744 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307744 1111 #uuid "fb3fb868-4570-5a9a-b52a-747182db6646" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307745 1034 "I only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307745 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [1759218630774