 $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307751 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307752 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307753 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307754 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307755 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1041 "selection" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307754 13194139534753 true] [17592186307750 1110 #uuid "48aa4abd-b180-5e8e-9816-3c7c5a88ffe0" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307751 1034 "-e" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307751 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534753 true] [17592186307751 1111 #uuid "d19cc507-1717-5bc1-b722-d7cf73bcca07" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307752 1034 "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307752 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307752 1111 #uuid "e9d5fa21-a465-5364-991e-0c1ca4c3d027" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307753 1034 "e" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307753 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534753 true] [17592186307753 1111 #uuid "747056ce-e799-5d02-8c4d-a27a1724760e" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307754 1034 "0" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307754 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534753 true] [17592186307754 1111 #uuid "bf465f11-4498-53b1-b90c-1c7909139642" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307755 1034 "{e}^{-1}" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307755 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534753 true] [17592186307755 1111 #uuid "886178e6-0d6a-55f2-b95c-bbcae5ac7e6a" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307756 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307757 13194139534753 true] [17592186307756 1089 "q-344401" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307756 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534753 true] [17592186307756 1100 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-{x}^{2}$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534753 true] [17592186307756 1113 #uuid "6bd80546-8633-537d-bf3f-1c4166a0651b" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307756 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-2x\\,=\\,2x(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}-1)\\,=\\,-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{2}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{(1)^{2}+1}=-\\frac{2}{2}=-1<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307757 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307758 13194139534753 true] [17592186307757 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534753 true] [17592186307757 1041 "field-1" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307757 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307758 13194139534753 true] [17592186307757 1110 #uuid "5b5506ca-5ab5-5e87-846b-480bc7ad94fa" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307758 1034 "0" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307758 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534753 true] [17592186307758 1111 #uuid "64d77bf0-76e9-5323-b000-308d4373b0e6" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307759 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307760 13194139534753 true] [17592186307759 1089 "q-49026" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307759 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534753 true] [17592186307759 1100 "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=3$ is a local minimum" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307759 1113 #uuid "f0d38c8a-0688-526d-84c1-a9ace67f2eb7" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307759 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x=0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{3}}{{e}^{-1}}=4e>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-(1)^{3}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{3(4)^{2}-(4)^{3}}{{e}^{4}}=-\\frac{16}{{e}^{4}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307761 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307762 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307763 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307764 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307765 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1041 "selection" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307765 13194139534753 true] [17592186307760 1110 #uuid "3440ced2-8f43-5053-bc60-f826dfc15ccc" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307761 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307761 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307761 1111 #uuid "23fcb0b0-3ffe-5ac2-aca0-fed04d4e131d" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307762 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307762 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307762 1111 #uuid "336891a4-d430-5c42-8084-cc47188b48a0" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307763 1034 "I only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307763 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307763 1111 #uuid "a5c056e3-7378-55ef-a1a4-6b037380aa3c" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307764 1034 "III only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307764 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307764 1111 #uuid "3662e3ac-6963-5cdf-9a68-50464e2afddd" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307765 1034 "II only" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307765 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534753 true] [17592186307765 1111 #uuid "015fc6fa-d299-5fc7-8189-f2e1ec50b962" 13194139534753 true] [17592186307766 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307767 131941395